q-34271" :question/id #uuid "b0234cd0-52d9-50d6-b3c1-2afa6f871100" :question/math-academy-id "q-34271" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-34271/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-34271"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291/selection/042526f0f149ecdc71a38ff369b428df94fe7b45e30f66bc4fcc4a3e4c26d45f" :answer/id #uuid "7722ec08-e07e-5035-8463-5614f640ebc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291/selection/51a095e0b68bf5e4f23f4ce05cd97d0463c784481999fd29715f89caae21911b" :answer/id #uuid "8f0f1fe3-3b0a-5643-8234-d64b88e8e8ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291/selection/e8057b6831062ce6ab13a510c53035204819425697a814aafca5df2df68ebcc4" :answer/id #uuid "47f2ebb1-61c0-5183-9497-0c3e35f78e63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291/selection/c0bb241511534a82ed83bf9f68ac61e0b32368db71a28bd7cf3b67bde4af7283" :answer/id #uuid "c7a2cad4-6c26-50ae-bc13-ab21969e02b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291/selection/6a145e4334efe339650b8b70a32aec06578133b46b600eab008353c52a2a70e3" :answer/id #uuid "75c3e248-a555-55be-94ce-d9ab0f9e5912" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcc5fcb-b78f-537c-9052-00d024f08b3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105291/selection/c0bb241511534a82ed83bf9f68ac61e0b32368db71a28bd7cf3b67bde4af7283" :answer-field/choices ["q-105291/selection/042526f0f149ecdc71a38ff369b428df94fe7b45e30f66bc4fcc4a3e4c26d45f" "q-105291/selection/51a095e0b68bf5e4f23f4ce05cd97d0463c784481999fd29715f89caae21911b" "q-105291/selection/e8057b6831062ce6ab13a510c53035204819425697a814aafca5df2df68ebcc4" "q-105291/selection/c0bb241511534a82ed83bf9f68ac61e0b32368db71a28bd7cf3b67bde4af7283" "q-105291/selection/6a145e4334efe339650b8b70a32aec06578133b46b600eab008353c52a2a70e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105291" :question/id #uuid "faa7ddb4-5b88-5e56-a53e-35fdb9d2ef93" :question/math-academy-id "q-105291" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-105291/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105291"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294/selection/fca4a282c24286c3ec925e17931060ef89404b9fe296f07b0ab0d4cbeafc4bea" :answer/id #uuid "ffc6e7ad-1c38-564f-8077-60bcebdb6543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294/selection/04d5d5c310141445ef2581089fd3edba25f0c1b1c9a29b8e8bcee2c61777ee88" :answer/id #uuid "be3806fe-85ce-56e4-a09c-bccae70e99eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294/selection/905ae8ee39048b9392c78f195eb6dfa97172546e65cfb5266a0bdd411c2676bf" :answer/id #uuid "fc97c3bf-6e13-5a63-a7de-4a504f3de464" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294/selection/e225c9aa78115453aa969ca449163a7e54c136534537cce5c2f2044a287dca4e" :answer/id #uuid "2d3b9bea-521b-5e47-84e1-3b2b9833bca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294/selection/a58e880e036264e5b21442a2e9867416c984cde73a96371bb168dcb66090a333" :answer/id #uuid "7003f756-dbbd-5b53-87ac-ee87febc742a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17f1b1ef-4f58-59c7-9e2b-3bdc4754ff24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105294/selection/e225c9aa78115453aa969ca449163a7e54c136534537cce5c2f2044a287dca4e" :answer-field/choices ["q-105294/selection/fca4a282c24286c3ec925e17931060ef89404b9fe296f07b0ab0d4cbeafc4bea" "q-105294/selection/04d5d5c310141445ef2581089fd3edba25f0c1b1c9a29b8e8bcee2c61777ee88" "q-105294/selection/905ae8ee39048b9392c78f195eb6dfa97172546e65cfb5266a0bdd411c2676bf" "q-105294/selection/e225c9aa78115453aa969ca449163a7e54c136534537cce5c2f2044a287dca4e" "q-105294/selection/a58e880e036264e5b21442a2e9867416c984cde73a96371bb168dcb66090a333"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105294" :question/id #uuid "c808f883-92bd-549d-ba16-96baa9b7cc6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105294" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=-2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-105294/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105294"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-157162/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "68ac05a0-7f61-5823-a916-d9b2d48c48e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157162/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f4cd8875-4cb6-5cb4-a120-5e6ff01b123f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-157162/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "53e671f3-a550-5598-b631-b71304487cbe" :answer/type :answer.typ